{
  "note": [
    "The Escape Pod seen from INSIDE THE SAFETY WEB (DR-112, the user's decision of 2026-09-16, asked for repeatedly before it was heard). The same cabin as ESCAPE-POD.json, cut from the one Feinstein layout in scripts/fabric-feinstein.mjs and moved so the nest's eye is at the origin -- regenerate from there, do not hand-edit.",
    "Why it is a room at all: the game moves the player INTO the safety web, which is a vehicle (SAFETY-WEB, VEHBIT and CLIMBBIT, globals.zil 950-956), and DR-112 keeps them there from the explosion to the thud -- the whole storyboard, the Feinstein blowing apart and the planet growing from four thousand miles to the surf, is read from this seat. That is a different eye from the one standing in the open strip, and the view system gives one eye and one ring to a room, so the nest gets its own. It is an ART room: `viewOf` says the game room it belongs to, and nothing in the rules changes.",
    "The eye is seated 1.2 m up, 0.5 m behind the near strap plane (z -0.55) and 1.45 m in front of the back curtain, square to the middle of the 2.4 x 1.1 m porthole 1.4 m across the cabin on the north flank. The near straps are the foreground and the view through them is nearly normal to the pane, which is the whole reason the window is opposite the webbing rather than on an end wall: a line to the end wall rakes the strap plane at 66 degrees and hides three quarters of what is beyond, where this one hides about a fifth.",
    "THE VIEW IS CLAMPED here, not free: a player strapped into a safety web cannot swing round, and the storyboard is the porthole. About 40 degrees either side of the pane's normal, which fits inside one painted view at lens 110 with margin -- so this room wants ONE frame and no ring or caps."
  ],
